# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, a checkout on a machine with
# core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default, and set on at least one real host)
# produces CRLF for every file here. Two things break, one of them silently:
#
#   1. A shell script COPYd into a Linux image gets a shebang of
#      `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and the container fails to start with a
#      bad-interpreter error naming a file that plainly exists.
#
#   2. `. ./.env` puts a trailing carriage return INSIDE the credential value, so
#      authentication fails with nothing visibly wrong with the file. This is the
#      nastier one - there is no error message pointing at line endings.
#
# The policy is deliberately ASYMMETRIC. Read the *.ps1 rule before changing it.

# Let git decide for text it has no specific rule about, rather than guessing.
* text=auto

# --- Must be LF: executed by a UNIX shell or inside a Linux container ---------
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# The guide's HTML is GENERATED by Node, which writes LF, and the test suite
# byte-compares the committed pages against a fresh generation to prove they have
# not drifted from activities.js. Without this rule a Windows checkout converts
# them to CRLF, the comparison fails, and the reported reason ("guide is out of
# date") is the one thing that is not actually wrong.
*.html      text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Extensionless shebang scripts. These carry no suffix to match on, so they are
# named individually - and anything added beside them must be added here too.
verify-setup.sh     text eol=lf
checkpoint.sh text eol=lf

# .env and its template hold credentials that are read character-for-character.
# A stray CR here is the silent auth failure described above.
.env          text eol=lf
.env.example  text eol=lf

# --- Must NOT be forced: PowerShell ------------------------------------------
# *.ps1 is deliberately absent from the LF list, and must stay absent.
#
# verify.ps1 runs on the HOST, where `powershell.exe` is Windows PowerShell 5.1.
# 5.1 decodes a BOM-less file as Windows-1252, so the UTF-8 BOM on these files is
# load-bearing - without it every non-ASCII character in a message becomes
# mojibake. Forcing eol or normalising these files risks the BOM.
# CRLF is also simply correct for PowerShell on Windows.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never touch -----------------------------------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.ttf       binary
*.woff2     binary

# NOTE for an existing clone made before this file landed: adding .gitattributes
# does not rewrite files already in your working tree. Re-clone, or run
#   git rm --cached -r . && git reset --hard
# to renormalise.
